st += [P('CHAPTER 1: INTRODUCTION','H1x'),P('1.1 Background','H2x'),P('Neonatal mortality remained a significant public-health challenge in Uganda and sub-Saharan Africa. Hypothermia and hypoxia required continuous observation, yet rural facilities often operated with limited staffing, intermittent electricity and unreliable connectivity. The project therefore implemented a low-cost, offline-first monitor that combined pulse oximetry, non-contact temperature measurement, local alarms, local storage and optional backend analysis.','Bodyx'),P('1.2 Problem Statement','H2x'),P('Rural clinics lacked affordable multi-parameter monitoring that continued to alert staff when Wi-Fi or cloud services were unavailable. The implemented system addressed this gap by keeping threshold alerts and data buffering on the ESP32-S3, while using the backend for history, risk scoring and dashboard visualization when connectivity returned.','Bodyx'),P('1.3 Main Objective','H2x'),P('The project designed, developed, tested and evaluated a low-cost offline-capable neonatal monitoring prototype that generated real-time local alerts for abnormal temperature and oxygenation, buffered readings locally, synchronized them when connectivity returned, and provided backend-assisted risk scoring and trend views.','Bodyx'),P('1.3.1 Specific Objectives','H2x'),P('<b>Hardware design:</b> The sensing, alert, display, timekeeping, storage and power interfaces were designed around the ESP32-S3. The tested display implementation used the ST7789 SPI screen; the earlier proposal LCD was replaced during implementation.','Bodyx'),P('<b>Software development:</b> Firmware, API, PostgreSQL data storage, the explainable AI prototype and React dashboard were implemented as one connected workflow.','Bodyx'),P('<b>Testing:</b> Hardware diagnostics, firmware builds, SD operations, API flows and end-to-end data handling were tested individually and together.','Bodyx'),P('<b>Evaluation:</b> Recorded test evidence was compared with the proposed performance measures, while AI outputs were treated as synthetic research estimates rather than clinical diagnoses.','Bodyx'),PageBreak()]
st += [P('CHAPTER 2: LITERATURE REVIEW','H1x'),P('The proposal established the need for continuous monitoring, offline operation and explainable warning. The implementation retained those requirements and made the safety boundary explicit: threshold alarms ran independently of the experimental model, and AI outputs were labelled synthetic and non-clinical. The final system therefore addressed the identified gap without treating a prototype model as a validated clinical instrument.','Bodyx'),P('CHAPTER 3: METHODOLOGY AND IMPLEMENTATION','H1x'),P('3.1 Overview of the Approach','H2x'),P('The work followed the Design, Develop, Test and Evaluate sequence from the proposal. Hardware connections were represented in a KiCad schematic before routing. Firmware was tested on the ESP32-S3, backend routes stored readings and alerts, the AI package trained on simulated data, and the dashboard consumed API responses rather than frontend sample data.','Bodyx'),P('3.2 Hardware Design','H2x'),P('The MAX30102 and MLX90614 shared the 3.3 V I2C bus on GPIO4 and GPIO5. The DS1302 used GPIO6, GPIO7 and GPIO14. The ST7789 used GPIO8, GPIO9, GPIO10, GPIO11 and GPIO12. The SD interface used GPIO21, GPIO40, GPIO41 and GPIO42. GPIO15 drove the buzzer driver; GPIO16, GPIO17 and GPIO18 drove the RGB channels through 330 ohm resistors. The schematic connected sensor power and returns to common ground and marked unused ESP32-S3 connections with no-connect symbols.','Bodyx'),P('The routed PCB used two copper layers, filled ground pours and stitching vias. The later onboard ESP32-S3 and USB-C/LiPo power design was documented as a separate schematic revision so it could be reviewed without overwriting the validated carrier board.','Bodyx')]
