st += [PageBreak(),P('3.8 Budget and Cost Analysis','H1x'),P('The proposal budget covered the ESP32, sensors, RTC, display, alarms, prototype wiring, USB power and optional SD storage. Implementation retained the low-cost module-based approach. The final onboard power revision added a USB-C connector, charger, LiPo cell and 3.3 V regulator, so the final procurement estimate required updating after the exact ESP32 module, battery capacity and display mechanics were selected.','Bodyx'),P('3.9 Ethical Considerations and Limitations','H1x'),P('Testing used student volunteers, bench checks and simulated data rather than neonates. The AI model was not clinically validated, did not constitute a diagnosis and was not presented as a clinical probability. Physical fit, sensor calibration, long-term battery safety, charge-current compatibility and hospital approval remained outside the demonstrated scope.','Bodyx'),P('CHAPTER 4: CONCLUSION AND RECOMMENDATIONS','H1x'),P('The project achieved a documented offline-first neonatal monitoring prototype. It connected the sensors and local alert devices to the ESP32-S3, implemented local storage and optional synchronization, provided API-backed dashboard views, and produced an explainable AI research path. The strongest evidence was the successful firmware build/upload, repeated SD diagnostic passes, connected schematic, routed PCB and zero reported KiCad rule/parity violations for the validated carrier revision.','Bodyx'),P('Before clinical or manufacturing use, the system should be retested with calibrated reference instruments, a controlled battery and charger qualification, a confirmed ESP32-S3 module antenna layout, measured sampling/latency datasets, and an independently reviewed AI evaluation dataset.','Bodyx'),P('REFERENCES','H1x'),P('The references from the proposal were retained. Additional implementation references included Espressif ESP32-S3 hardware design guidance, the Texas Instruments BQ24074 datasheet for 1-cell power-path charging, and the TPS63001 datasheet for single-cell buck-boost regulation.','Bodyx')]
st += [PageBreak(),P('PERSONAL MOTIVATION AND FUTURE LEARNING IN FRANCE','H1x'),P('This project represented more than a classroom prototype: it was a practical demonstration of my passion for artificial intelligence, embedded systems and socially useful engineering. I wanted to build a device that could continue working in resource-limited settings, while also learning how data, software and hardware could be combined responsibly. Studying in France would allow me to deepen this foundation through advanced courses, laboratories and collaboration in embedded computing, machine learning, signal processing, medical technology and responsible AI. I would bring the discipline developed through this project—careful testing, transparent limitations and a willingness to learn—and use it to contribute to research and engineering teams.','Bodyx'),P('The project also showed me what I still need to master: calibrated biomedical sensing, robust model validation, low-power electronics, secure connected systems and deployment with clinicians. France would give me the academic environment and mentorship to turn this prototype into a more rigorous, safe and generalisable system.','Bodyx'),P('FULL SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE','H1x'),P('The architecture below was used to explain how bedside sensing, the backend API, the dashboard and the AI risk-modeling components work together.','Bodyx'),Image(r'C:/Users/AKENA/AppData/Local/Temp/codex-clipboard-19670609-5b18-4aac-80d7-1ebfcdb00541.png',width=175*mm,height=158*mm),P('Figure 4.1. Full NeoGuard application and AI architecture supplied for the pitch report.','Smallx')]
